   "source": [
    "K_GRID = [3, 5, 8, 10, 15]\n",
    "C_GRID = [0.01, 0.1, 0.3, 1.0, 10.0]\n",
    "\n",
    "rows = []\n",
    "for k in K_GRID:\n",
    "    for C in C_GRID:\n",
    "        s = group_oof_scores(build_logreg(k, C), X_tr, y_tr, g_tr)\n",
    "        rows.append({\"k\": k, \"C\": C,\n",
    "                     \"roc_auc\": round(roc_auc_score(y_tr, s), 3),\n",
    "                     \"avg_precision\": round(average_precision_score(y_tr, s), 3)})\n",
    "\n",
    "sweep = pd.DataFrame(rows)\n",
    "print(\"ROC-AUC  (k = rows, C = columns)\")\n",
    "display(sweep.pivot(index=\"k\", columns=\"C\", values=\"roc_auc\"))\n",
    "print(\"average precision\")\n",
    "display(sweep.pivot(index=\"k\", columns=\"C\", values=\"avg_precision\"))\n",
    "sweep.sort_values(\"roc_auc\", ascending=False).head(5)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ec0ce187",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 7b. Best configuration, in detail\n",
    "\n",
    "Average precision is the more honest single number here: ROC-AUC is flattered by\n",
    "a large negative pool, whereas AP punishes a model that ranks negatives near the\n",
    "top. Note also that with one fold per actor, these numbers move a lot when a\n",
    "single actor is added or removed."
   ]
  },
